WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:08.388
Organic chemistry is based on carbon containing molecules with CH bonds,

00:00:08.389 --> 00:00:15.799
so some joke that organic chemists see the entire periodic table as carbon except for a few other key

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:18.979
elements. Now, the stereotype kind of makes sense.

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:26.658
Organic chemistry arose as a discipline to describe the chemistry of life and the elements contained within the human body are

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:35.119
concentrated in this upper right portion of the periodic table, but if we're talking about biochemistry, there are other elements that are important as well.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.799
Trace elements in the human body include the transition metals, and

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:47.239
many of the reactants that you'll meet in organic chemistry 1 &amp; 2 span the entire periodic table. All

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.930
that being said, I'm going to give into stereotypes for a moment

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:58.099
and we're going to focus on the bonding of elements in the upper right-hand portion of the periodic table because these are the most likely

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:04.819
eements to be bonded to carbon in organic molecules. Let's briefly review the octet rule.

00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:09.589
Most elements like to have eight electrons around them.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.640
This includes all of the electrons that are shared in

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.499
bonds. There are many exceptions to this, of course, and a notable one is hydrogen,

00:01:18.899 --> 00:01:25.248
which only makes one bond and therefore has two electrons around itself. If you're not allowed to use a

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:27.329
periodic table to complete your exam,

00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:33.468
it's wise to memorize the periodic table going over from carbon to fluorine and down the halogens.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.749
You can use my silly acronym: Can Normal Owls Fly, or come up with your own.

00:01:39.719 --> 00:01:45.049
Going from left to right the number of valence electrons that the element prefers to have

00:01:45.779 --> 00:01:48.619
increases, and now we get to our little trick.

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:56.809
Starting with carbon we can count down from four and we get the number of bonds that the element likes to form when it is

00:01:57.119 --> 00:01:58.200
neutral.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.310
Now phosphorus and sulfur also follow this rule

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.310
but they're special cases

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:09.889
because they have empty d orbitals they can actually exceed an octet of electrons around them.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:18.500
So phosphorus, when neutral, will form either three or five bonds and sulfur can form two, four, or six

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.559
bonds to other atoms when neutral.

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:27.919
Let's look at a simple example of a molecule containing an element from each group

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:34.699
so we can correlate the number of valence electrons with a number of bonds that the element likes to make.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:42.679
Here I've drawn out methane with a pair of dots to represent the two electrons that are shared in the bond to hydrogen. In

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:49.069
organic chemistry, it's more conventional to show the pair of electrons as a single line

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.269
connecting the two atoms.

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:55.519
Keep in mind that each of these lines is representing a pair of electrons.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.400
Now, to count valence electrons,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:06.260
we can think about splitting the bond in half and thinking carbon "owns" half of the electrons in each bond.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:14.780
Since carbon likes to have four valence electrons carbon is neutral in methane. Now, let's move on to ammonia, NH3.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:22.999
We see that nitrogen likes to make three bonds when it's neutral and we have three bonds to hydrogen in the structure of ammonia.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:29.839
However, nitrogen likes to have five valence electrons, and we only have three coming from bonds.

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:35.569
This is because nitrogen contains a lone pair—when an atom has a lone pair

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:42.500
you count the lone pair as belonging just to that atom itself, and so we have five valence electrons on nitrogen.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.140
We can look at the structure of water to explore the valence of oxygen.

00:03:48.959 --> 00:03:55.939
Oxygen is neutral when it makes two bonds so bonded to these two hydrogen atoms, we have a neutral molecule of water.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:03.769
But its valence is six. This is because oxygen has two lone pairs, so in water

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:12.380
oxygen is the "owner" of four electrons from lone pairs and half of the bonds to hydrogen. That's six valence electrons.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:20.690
For our fluorine example we're going to look at hydrofluoric acid. This is a weak acid, but a really toxic molecule.

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:26.598
Fluorine likes to make one bond when neutral but it also likes to have seven valence electrons,

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.240
so fluorine gets three lone pairs.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:37.199
This gives it six electrons in lone pairs plus one from the bond to hydrogen. This is seven valence electrons.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:44.309
If we simplify our periodic table even more to remove the left and the right hand groups,

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:51.179
we can come up with a trick to figure out the formal charge on an element. The number of bonds the element likes to form

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.849
when neutral is still written across the top of the table.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:05:00.419
Now, if the element is making one extra bond it is going to be positively charged,

00:05:00.419 --> 00:05:05.519
and if it's making one less bond it's going to be negatively charged.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:11.280
Let's look at water once again as our neutral example. Water wants to have six valence electrons

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:19.470
and it has four coming from the lone pair and two coming from bonds. The hydronium ion is a better way to represent

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:21.520
H+, or a

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.520
proton, in an acidic solution.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.579
Now we have three bonds to oxygen

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.579
but we don't want to break the octet rule

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:35.699
We still only want to have eight total electrons around our central element.

00:05:36.219 --> 00:05:41.999
So now oxygen has only one lone pair and a formal positive charge on oxygen.

00:05:43.419 --> 00:05:46.709
Okay, let's count our valence electrons to see how we figure this out.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:52.199
We already said we have six valence electrons in water. Now with the hydronium ion,

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:55.258
we have two electrons coming from lone pairs.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:02.159
We also have three electrons coming from the six electrons that are shared in bonds. Remember,

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.419
oxygen "owns" half of the electrons in the bond. If

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:12.239
we add this up, we see that oxygen has five valence electrons total. Well,

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.039
this is one less negative charge than the six valence electrons

00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:22.949
It likes to have when neutral. So, if we have one less negative—one less electron—

00:06:24.069 --> 00:06:26.069
oxygen has a formal positive charge.

00:06:26.349 --> 00:06:34.049
Alright, so we looked at an example where oxygen is making three bonds—one extra bond—and therefore has positive charge.

00:06:34.569 --> 00:06:39.838
Let's look at an example where it's making one less bond and has a formal negative charge.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:42.580
Hydroxide is a

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:46.499
deprotonated form of water that exists in basic solutions.

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:51.809
Let's count electrons. We have just one electron coming from the bond to hydrogen.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:59.730
Oxygen is only sharing electrons in one bond, so to complete its octet we need to add three lone pairs.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:08.070
With the one electron from the shared bond, and the six electrons from lone pairs, we get seven valence electrons

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:09.880
on oxygen.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:16.740
This is one more electron than oxygen likes to have when neutral. If we have one extra negative charge,

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:20.339
we get a formal minus charge on oxygen.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:25.920
Let's return to our original piece of the periodic table for a moment that we were looking at.

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:33.119
This formal charge trick works for all of the elements in the middle three groups,

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:39.509
so it will work for nitrogen and phosphorous, oxygen and sulfur, and the halogens.

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:42.929
But it doesn't work for carbon. Let's see why.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:46.020
Carbon likes to have four groups around it. I'm

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:48.570
going to label these groups "R".

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:55.050
This is a convention used in organic chemistry when we don't want to specifically define what the group is.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:01.199
Now if we think about losing "R" but leaving its electrons behind on

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:04.320
carbon we get an extra lone pair on carbon.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:11.280
This gives carbon five valence electrons, therefore the atom carries a formal negative charge.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:16.469
Now if we think about carbon ionizing by losing the R group

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:20.759
with a pair of electrons we get a different story.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.570
Carbon now has just three valence electrons.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:31.710
It wants to have four, so it's missing one negative charge and it takes on a formal positive charge

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:38.490
itself. This species is called a carbocation, and it ALSO has three bonds.

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:43.830
So you can see why our formal charge trick doesn't quite work with carbon. In

00:08:44.229 --> 00:08:49.799
this lesson, we looked at the valence of atoms that are most likely to be bonded to carbon in

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:51.730
organic molecules.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:53.530
We looked at some simple examples

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.530
and figured out how to determine

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:58.920
how many lone pairs are on each atom.

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:04.589
We also looked at a trick for figuring out formal charge on the groups to the right of carbon and

00:09:04.839 --> 00:09:08.219
saw why this trick can't be applied to carbon itself.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.649
We'll look at the bonding of many more elements and practice Lewis structures in another lesson.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:19.829
KP here. If you learned something give me a thumbs up on the way out and for more chemistry subscribe to my channel.

